<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Geneva</h1><p class="st-meta">1535 CE – 1798 CE · capital Geneva</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Calvinist city-republic on Lake Geneva, the 'Protestant Rome', which threw off its bishop and Savoy in 1535.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> was ruled by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-geneva">Prince-Bishopric of Geneva</a>, with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy</a> exerting growing influence. The townspeople allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-fribourg">Canton of Fribourg</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-bern">Canton of Bern</a> in 1526, and in 1535, with Bernese support, they drove out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, making the city an independent republic.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a>, who settled there in 1536 and again from 1541, shaped its church ordinances; the Consistory enforced strict morals, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academy-of-geneva">Academy</a>, founded in 1559, trained Reformed pastors for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Councils dominated by leading families governed, and the city prospered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>, banking and watchmaking, much of it brought by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenot</a> refugees. In 1602 Genevans repelled Savoy's night attack, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/l-escalade">Escalade</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in 1798, making it the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leman-department">Léman</a>. Freed in 1813, it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation</a> as a canton in 1815.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-geneva.jpg" alt="Republic of Geneva" width="320" height="321"><figcaption>Image: Original: Werner Luzi for Louis Mühlemann (1977) Vector: Swiss Army · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Canton_of_Geneva.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1535 CE – 1798 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Geneva</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Reformed Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Geneva, République de Genève</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1666&amp;focus=republic-of-geneva" aria-label="Republic of Geneva on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4825 552 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Geneva lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5145 808L5145 808M5145 808L5186 782M5145 808L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5145 808h0M5186 782h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5145 808h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5145 808h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Geneva. <a href="/atlas?year=1666&amp;focus=republic-of-geneva">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
